 974		\delta_{\mathrm{Line}}^{1/2}))$, obtained by chaining the three claims
 975		(Lemmas~\ref{lem:claim-17-1}--\ref{lem:claim-17-3}), and
 976		$O(m^2 \delta_P)$, obtained by decomposing the left-hand side of
 977		\eqref{eq:qld-combined-lines-consistency} into restricted-distribution
 978		terms and bounding each by \eqref{eq:qld-xz-lines-restricted}; a
 979		sentence is also duplicated at the corresponding point of the source,
 980		the second copy with a malformed reference. The second route requires
 981		the joint law of the pair $((\ell_X, x), (\ell_Z, z))$, for
 982		$(\ell, \ell_X, \ell_Z) \sim D$ and $(x,z,\alpha,\beta)$ uniform on
 983		$\ell$, to be a mixture of the product distributions
 984		$D_{\mathrm{v}_1,i} \times D_{\mathrm{v}_2,j}$, since
 985		\eqref{eq:qld-xz-lines-restricted} averages over two independently
 986		sampled line-point pairs; Property~2 of Lemma~\ref{lem:qld-sublines}
 987		determines only the two marginal laws of $(\ell_X, \ell_Z, x)$ and of
 988		$(\ell_X, \ell_Z, z)$ separately, not the joint law of
 989		$((\ell_X, x), (\ell_Z, z))$, so the decomposition invoked by the
 990		source is not established there. The proof above therefore uses only
 991		the first route. Neither route, moreover, delivers the error form
 992		$\poly(m^2\eps, md/q)$ claimed in the source. The first route proves
 993		the bound $O(m(\eps^{1/4} + \delta_P^{1/4} + \delta_Q^{1/4} +
 994		\delta_{\mathrm{Line}}^{1/2}))$, a function of the form
 995		$m \cdot \poly(\eps, md/q)$, and no function of $m^2\eps$ and $md/q$
 996		alone that vanishes as its arguments vanish dominates it: at
