158		% (references/qpbt-paper/08_classical_and_quantum_low_degree_tests.tex:446-454).
159		% After the zero-direction correction of Definition def:ld-win-predicate
160		% (see rem:ld-win-zero-direction) the identity holds only away from
161		% zero-direction diagonal questions, so the import is scoped to the predicate
162		% analyzed in the external source and bridged by the probability estimate below,
163		% which is insensitive to the external test's acceptance convention on that event.
164		The two-prover tensor-code test for the code $\code^{\ot m}$, as described in Figures~1 and~2 of~\cite{Ji2021Quantum}, agrees with the game $\mathcal G^{\mathrm{ld}}$ with parameters $\mathsf{ldparams}$ and $k=1$ on every question pair whose diagonal-line direction is nonzero: the axis-parallel line-versus-point test is their axis-parallel lines test, with the roles of the two players chosen uniformly at random, which is consistent with the two-prover variant of the tensor-code test from Section~4.1 there; the diagonal line-versus-point test is their subcube commutation test, again with the added symmetrization between the players; and the consistency test is their synchronicity test. On diagonal line-versus-point questions with direction $v' = \pi_{i-1}(v) = 0$, the win predicate of Definition~\ref{def:ld-win-predicate} resolves an ambiguity of the source (Remark~\ref{rem:ld-win-zero-direction}), and the resolution is not required to match the acceptance convention of the subcube commutation test on this event: the event has probability at most $2/(mq)$ under the question distribution, since the direction $\pi_{i-1}(v)$ vanishes with probability $\frac1m \sum_{j=1}^m q^{-j} \le \frac{1}{m(q-1)} \le \frac{2}{mq}$ over uniform $(s,v)$, so the success probabilities of any fixed strategy in the two games differ by at most $2/(mq)$. A projective strategy that succeeds in $\mathcal G^{\mathrm{ld}}$ with probability at least $1-\eps$ therefore succeeds in the tensor-code test with probability at least $1 - \eps - 2/(mq)$. Theorem~4.7 of~\cite{Ji2021Quantum}, instantiated with its internal parameter $k$ chosen as $m^3 d$ (this parameter is unrelated to the simultaneity parameter $k$ of $\mathsf{ldparams}$) and applied with the success deficit $\eps + 2/(mq)$ in place of $\eps$, then yields the present statement for $k = 1$: since $0 < b \le 1$ and $m \ge 1$ give $(\eps + 2/(mq))^b \le \eps^b + 2\,q^{-b}$, the additional error is absorbed into $\delta_{\mathrm{ld}}$ by enlarging the universal constant $a$, using $dmk \ge 1$. The case of general $k$ follows by a standard reduction, following exactly the derivation of Theorem~4.43 from Theorem~4.40 in~\cite{Natarajan2018NEEXP}. Quantum soundness of the low individual degree test itself is the result of~\cite{Ji2020LowIndividualDegree}; Remark~\ref{rem:ld-soundness-provider} relates the present statement to Theorem~\ref{thm:main-formal}, which proves that result.
165	\end{proof}
