 107	\begin{remark}[Diagonal questions with zero direction]\label{rem:ld-win-zero-direction}
 108		The source states the two line-versus-point clauses with ``the'' $t \in \F_q$ such that $x_{\overline w} = u_0 + t v$. When the direction is nonzero and $x_{\overline w}$ lies on the line, exactly one such $t$ exists and the formulation above agrees with the source. But zero directions occur: Definition~\ref{def:line} explicitly permits them, and the question distribution of Definition~\ref{def:ld-question-distribution} produces $\mathsf{DLine}$ questions with $v' = \pi_{i-1}(v) = 0$ with positive probability, namely whenever the last $m - i + 1$ coordinates of $v$ vanish. In that case $\ell(u_0, 0) = \{u_0\}$, every $t \in \F_q$ satisfies $x_{\overline w} = u_0 + t v'$ when $x_{\overline w} = u_0$, and the source clause does not determine acceptance for a nonconstant answer polynomial. The universal quantification over $t$ adopted in Definition~\ref{def:ld-win-predicate} makes the predicate a well-defined function of its arguments; it accepts vacuously when no such $t$ exists, a case the question distribution never produces. Completeness is unaffected: the restriction of a polynomial $g$ to the singleton line $\ell(u_0,0)$, expressed through the parameterization $t \mapsto u_0 + t \cdot 0$, is the constant polynomial with value $g(u_0)$, which passes the check for every $t$. Soundness is likewise unaffected: zero directions occur with probability at most $2/(mq)$, and the proof of Theorem~\ref{lem:ld-soundness} shows that any acceptance convention on this event changes success probabilities by at most that amount, which is absorbed into the universal constants of $\delta_{\mathrm{ld}}$.
 109	\end{remark}
 110	
 111	The soundness properties of the low individual degree game are expressed in terms of the following class of measurements.
 112	
 113	\begin{definition}[Low-degree polynomial measurements]\label{def:ld-meas}
 114		\uses{def:polyfunc, def:polymeasurements, def:polynomials-degree}
 115		Let $\polymeas{m}{q}{d}$ denote the set of POVM measurements indexed by $\polyfunc{m}{q}{d}$, the polynomial representatives of individual degree at most $d$ in each variable; this is the notion of Definition~\ref{def:polymeasurements}, and the argument order $(m,q,d)$ follows that definition (the paper orders the arguments as $(m,d,q)$). By Definition~\ref{def:polynomials-degree}, evaluation identifies these representatives with the corresponding polynomial functions $g\colon \F_q^m \to \F_q$, bijectively whenever $d \le q-1$; the parameter regimes of this chapter (Definition~\ref{def:introparams} fixes $d = 1$, and admissibility gives $q \ge 2$) always satisfy this. More generally, for an integer $k$ and tuples $m = (m_1,\ldots,m_k)$, $q = (q_1,\ldots,q_k)$, $d = (d_1,\ldots,d_k)$, let $\mathrm{PolyMeas}(m,q,d,k)$ denote the set of measurements $G = \{G_{g_1,\ldots,g_k}\}$ such that for each $i \in \{1,\ldots,k\}$, the outcome index $g_i$ is a polynomial $g_i\colon \F_{q_i}^{m_i} \to \F_{q_i}$ of individual degree at most $d_i$.
 116	\end{definition}
 117	
 118	Quantum soundness of a version of the classical low-degree test for polynomials of low \emph{total} degree was claimed by Natarajan and Vidick, building on an analysis of a three-prover version of the test by Vidick; there is a known gap in the soundness analysis of that test, and it is not used here. Instead, the construction relies on the low \emph{individual} degree test, whose quantum soundness is proved in~\cite{Ji2020LowIndividualDegree} and generalized in~\cite{Ji2021Quantum}. The statement below is the form most directly useful in what follows.
 119	
 120	% The paper states this result in a theorem environment with the label lem:ld-soundness.
 121	% We keep both the environment and the label verbatim for cross-reference fidelity.
 122	\begin{theorem}[Quantum soundness of the simultaneous classical low individual degree test]\label{lem:ld-soundness}
 123		\uses{def:ld-game, def:ld-question-distribution, def:ld-win-predicate, def:ld-meas, def:projective-strategy-general, def:tensor-product-value, def:povm-distance, def:bracket}
 124		There exists a function
 125		\[
 126			\delta_{\mathrm{ld}}(\eps, q, m, d, k) = a\,(dmk)^a \bigl(\eps^b + q^{-b} + 2^{-bmd}\bigr)
 127		\]
 128		for universal constants $a \ge 1$ and $0 < b \le 1$ such that the following holds. For all $\eps > 0$ and every parameter tuple $\mathsf{ldparams} = (q,m,d,k)$ as in Definition~\ref{def:ld-game} (so $m, d, k \ge 1$, $q$ is an admissible field size, and $m$ divides $q$), and for all projective strategies $(\psi, A, B)$ that succeed with probability at least $1-\eps$ in the game $\mathcal G^{\mathrm{ld}}_{\mathsf{ldparams}}$, there exist measurements
 129		\[
 130			G^w \in \mathrm{PolyMeas}(m,q,d,k)
 131		\]
 132		on $\hilb_w$ for $w \in \{\mathrm A,\mathrm B\}$, where by slight abuse of notation $m$, $q$, $d$ denote the constant tuples $(m,\ldots,m)$, $(q,\ldots,q)$, $(d,\ldots,d)$ of length $k$, such that
 133		\begin{align*}
 134			A^{\mathsf{Point},\, u}_{a_1,\ldots,a_k} \ot I_{\mathrm B}
 135			&\approx_{\delta_{\mathrm{ld}}}
 136			I_{\mathrm A} \ot G^{\mathrm B}_{[(g_1(u),\ldots,g_k(u)) = (a_1,\ldots,a_k)]}\;,\\
 137			G^{\mathrm A}_{[(g_1(u),\ldots,g_k(u)) = (a_1,\ldots,a_k)]} \ot I_{\mathrm B}
 138			&\approx_{\delta_{\mathrm{ld}}}
 139			I_{\mathrm A} \ot B^{\mathsf{Point},\, u}_{a_1,\ldots,a_k}\;,\\
 140			G^{\mathrm A}_{g_1,\ldots,g_k} \ot I_{\mathrm B}
 141			&\approx_{\delta_{\mathrm{ld}}}
 142			I_{\mathrm A} \ot G^{\mathrm B}_{g_1,\ldots,g_k}\;,
 143		\end{align*}
 144		where $\delta_{\mathrm{ld}} = \delta_{\mathrm{ld}}(\eps,q,m,d,k)$, all three approximations are with respect to the state $\ket{\psi}$, and the bracket notation for post-processed outcomes is that of Definition~\ref{def:bracket}. The first two approximations hold on average over the uniform distribution on $u \in \F_q^m$; no question distribution is associated with the third.
 145	\end{theorem}
 146	\begin{proof}
 147		\uses{def:ld-question-distribution, def:ld-win-predicate, def:ld-meas, def:povm-distance}
 148		Let $\Sigma = \F_q$ and let $\code \subseteq \Sigma^q$ be the Reed--Solomon code of degree $d$: the set of all $(p(x_1),\ldots,p(x_q))$ where $p\colon \F_q \to \F_q$ is a univariate polynomial of degree at most $d$ and $x_1,\ldots,x_q$ enumerate $\F_q$. We may assume $d < q$. In the opposite regime the statement is immediate: for $d \ge q$ the prefactor gives $\delta_{\mathrm{ld}} \ge a\,(dmk)^a\, q^{-b} \ge q^{a-b} \ge 1$, using $m, k \ge 1$, while the quantity bounded in Definition~\ref{def:povm-distance} never exceeds $2$ for families of POVM elements, so the three approximations hold for an arbitrary choice of $G^w \in \mathrm{PolyMeas}(m,q,d,k)$. For $d < q$, evaluation is injective on polynomials of degree at most $d$ and a nonzero polynomial of degree at most $d$ vanishes at no more than $d$ of the $q$ points, so $\code$ is a linear $[q,\, d+1,\, q-d]_\Sigma$ code.
 149		% The paper prints the parameters as [q, d+1, n-d+1] with an undefined n. With the
 150		% intended value n = q the printed distance q-d+1 exceeds the true minimum distance
 151		% q-d of the degree-d evaluation code, and the dimension claim d+1 requires d < q.
 152		% Both points are corrected above; the regime d >= q, which the parameter tuple of
 153		% Definition def:ld-game permits, is handled by the triviality argument. The
 154		% boundary values d = 0 and k = 0 are excluded by the parameter domain of
 155		% Definition def:ld-game, following the source's convention that \N denotes
 156		% the positive integers.
 157		% The source asserts a flat identity between the two tests
 158		% (references/qpbt-paper/08_classical_and_quantum_low_degree_tests.tex:446-454).
 159		% After the zero-direction correction of Definition def:ld-win-predicate
 160		% (see rem:ld-win-zero-direction) the identity holds only away from
 161		% zero-direction diagonal questions, so the import is scoped to the predicate
 162		% analyzed in the external source and bridged by the probability estimate below,
 163		% which is insensitive to the external test's acceptance convention on that event.
 164		The two-prover tensor-code test for the code $\code^{\ot m}$, as described in Figures~1 and~2 of~\cite{Ji2021Quantum}, agrees with the game $\mathcal G^{\mathrm{ld}}$ with parameters $\mathsf{ldparams}$ and $k=1$ on every question pair whose diagonal-line direction is nonzero: the axis-parallel line-versus-point test is their axis-parallel lines test, with the roles of the two players chosen uniformly at random, which is consistent with the two-prover variant of the tensor-code test from Section~4.1 there; the diagonal line-versus-point test is their subcube commutation test, again with the added symmetrization between the players; and the consistency test is their synchronicity test. On diagonal line-versus-point questions with direction $v' = \pi_{i-1}(v) = 0$, the win predicate of Definition~\ref{def:ld-win-predicate} resolves an ambiguity of the source (Remark~\ref{rem:ld-win-zero-direction}), and the resolution is not required to match the acceptance convention of the subcube commutation test on this event: the event has probability at most $2/(mq)$ under the question distribution, since the direction $\pi_{i-1}(v)$ vanishes with probability $\frac1m \sum_{j=1}^m q^{-j} \le \frac{1}{m(q-1)} \le \frac{2}{mq}$ over uniform $(s,v)$, so the success probabilities of any fixed strategy in the two games differ by at most $2/(mq)$. A projective strategy that succeeds in $\mathcal G^{\mathrm{ld}}$ with probability at least $1-\eps$ therefore succeeds in the tensor-code test with probability at least $1 - \eps - 2/(mq)$. Theorem~4.7 of~\cite{Ji2021Quantum}, instantiated with its internal parameter $k$ chosen as $m^3 d$ (this parameter is unrelated to the simultaneity parameter $k$ of $\mathsf{ldparams}$) and applied with the success deficit $\eps + 2/(mq)$ in place of $\eps$, then yields the present statement for $k = 1$: since $0 < b \le 1$ and $m \ge 1$ give $(\eps + 2/(mq))^b \le \eps^b + 2\,q^{-b}$, the additional error is absorbed into $\delta_{\mathrm{ld}}$ by enlarging the universal constant $a$, using $dmk \ge 1$. The case of general $k$ follows by a standard reduction, following exactly the derivation of Theorem~4.43 from Theorem~4.40 in~\cite{Natarajan2018NEEXP}. Quantum soundness of the low individual degree test itself is the result of~\cite{Ji2020LowIndividualDegree}; Remark~\ref{rem:ld-soundness-provider} relates the present statement to Theorem~\ref{thm:main-formal}, which proves that result.
 165	\end{proof}
 166	
 167	\begin{remark}[Relation to the low individual degree test of Chapter~\ref{chap:test}]\label{rem:ld-soundness-provider}
 168		Theorem~\ref{lem:ld-soundness} is imported: the proof above reduces it to Theorem~4.7 of~\cite{Ji2021Quantum}, instantiated with its internal parameter $k$ chosen as $m^3 d$, followed by the extension to simultaneous $k$-tuples of~\cite{Natarajan2018NEEXP}. The result of~\cite{Ji2020LowIndividualDegree} that underlies it is proved here as Theorem~\ref{thm:main-formal}: for every projective strategy passing the $(m,q,d)$ low individual degree test of Definition~\ref{def:low-individual-degree-test} with probability at least $1-\eps$, that theorem produces measurements in $\polymeas{m}{q}{d}$ satisfying consistency relations of the same shape as the three approximations of Theorem~\ref{lem:ld-soundness}. Theorem~\ref{lem:ld-soundness} is not a consequence of Theorem~\ref{thm:main-formal} alone: a derivation from it would require, in addition, an equivalence between the typed game $\mathcal G^{\mathrm{ld}}_{(q,m,d,1)}$ and the $(m,q,d)$ test of Chapter~\ref{chap:test} --- matching the two question distributions, the two win conditions, and the two error functions --- together with the extension from $k = 1$ to general $k$ by the reduction of~\cite{Natarajan2018NEEXP} cited above. Neither implication is proved in this chapter, and the proof of Theorem~\ref{lem:ld-soundness} given above does not invoke Theorem~\ref{thm:main-formal}.
 169	\end{remark}
